// NEGATIVE TEST — `==` on a tuple (#89). A comparison operator applies only to
// a type with a comparison: an integer, a float, a bool or a char (PD0079).
//
// No requirement row and no specification text defines equality on a tuple
// (N5-10 is witnessed over the primitives only), and code generation emits C's
// own `==` between the operands: a tuple is a C struct, so on d5166e8 this
// program type-checked and gcc refused it ("invalid operands to binary ==").
// Refused by name now, with the way to compare instead (element by element);
// no structural equality is invented.
fn main() {
    let a = (1, 2);
    let b = (1, 2);
    if a == b {
        print_int(1);
    } else {
        print_int(0);
    }
}
